# Environment configuration for the portfolio.
#
# Copy to .env for your own reference, or set these directly in your shell
# and in the deployment provider's environment settings. The application
# reads real environment variables; .env itself is git-ignored and is never
# required for the site to run.
#
# This site has no API keys, no database, and no third-party credentials.
# The one secret it does have is shared between two deployments this
# repository owns, and is described at the bottom of this file. Real values
# belong in the platform's environment settings, never here.

# development (default) | production
#   development: /docs, /redoc and /openapi.json are served, no HSTS header.
#   production:  those three are not served, and HSTS is sent. Set this only
#                where the platform terminates HTTPS.
APP_ENV=development

# The shared secret the Vercel front door sends with every request it
# proxies, and the only thing that tells this app a request arrived through
# Vercel rather than straight at its Cloud Run URL. That distinction decides
# which X-Forwarded-For entry the rate limiter counts, so the value must be
# the same in both places:
#
#   Vercel:    the project's FRONT_DOOR_SECRET environment variable, which
#              vercel/build.mjs reads when it generates the routing table
#   Cloud Run: FRONT_DOOR_SECRET on the service
#
# Unset -- local development, and the tests -- means no request is treated as
# proxied twice, which is the safe default rather than a broken one. Rotate
# by setting a new value in both places; there is no window where a stale
# value is believed, only one where the front door is counted as a direct
# request.
FRONT_DOOR_SECRET=
